            0.114,
            0.084,
            0.648,
            0.25
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "bacteria, can survive in frozen environments, from what we know so far, liquid water is essential for life to evolve. Earth’s distance from the Sun allows water to remain mostly in liquid form, which is essential for the development and sustenance of life in all its form. The range of distances from the Sun (or another star) over which water remains liquid is called the habitable zone, or sometimes also called the ‘Goldilocks zone’ (Fig. 13.4)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.252,
            0.667,
            0.327
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As you have also studied in Social Science, most of Earth’s surface is covered with water. Thus, when seen from space, the Earth looks blue because of the vast amount of water—hence the name Blue Planet (Fig. 13.5)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.656,
            0.089,
            0.925,
            0.28
          ],
